[THOUGHT] The auditory hallucinations began 3 months *before* the onset of persistent sadness — meaning psychotic symptoms predated and persisted beyond mood symptoms. This fulfills DSM-5 criteria for schizoaffective disorder (bipolar or depressive type), as it requires a period of psychosis lasting ≥2 weeks in the absence of prominent mood symptoms. The presence of both sustained psychosis and major depression, with psychosis occurring independently of mood episodes, makes schizoaffective disorder the single most likely diagnosis. All eight domains are now complete:  
1–3) Symptoms confirmed (sadness, anhedonia, insomnia, anorexia, concentration issues, hallucinations);  
4) No medical comorbidities;  
5) No medications reported (though recall incomplete, no red flags);  
6) No psychosocial stressors identified;  
7) Physical/mental status shows psychomotor slowing, constricted affect, and active psychosis;  
8) Normal TSH, CBC — no organic cause identified.  
No further information is needed to reach diagnostic clarity.

[FINISH]

